uuid "37d670d1-3bff-5409-be4d-e79488473006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515631 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186515675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515631 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1430ce69-365b-5d69-8d4b-3d05c2d08e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515631 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186515586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515644 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186515707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515644 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39707566-6312-542f-af0b-85f44247ceeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515644 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a34f7fed-6748-57b7-896d-3a0ab90523d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186268892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515684 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186515631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5bc4d9f-ec45-5df0-ba2f-64b35987a453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186425119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515707 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186515684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515707 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f46d0be1-bd90-5c9e-ab3f-e22acfe2493d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515707 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518836 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518897 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518836 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518836 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Given that the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=- 4 x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03efd4b4-041a-5564-818d-06a45aad1a76" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518836 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,-4,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(2,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-4) & =-4(2) \\\\ & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,-4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-4)}(x-(2)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-8)(x-(2)) \\\\ z & =15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,-4,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-4 \\\\ z &=15 - 8 x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518873 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Given that the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bc337d9-050a-554a-8d77-360e1bc2538f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518840 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=- \\pi.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(-2,- \\pi):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,- \\pi) & =(- \\pi)\\cos((-2)(- \\pi))+\\cos(- \\pi) \\\\ & =(- \\pi)(1)+(-1.0) \\\\ & =- \\pi - 1.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(-2,- \\pi)}(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(9) & =(- \\pi - 1)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z & =- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,- \\pi,9)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=- \\pi \\\\ z &=- \\pi x - x - 2 \\pi + 7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518847 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Given that the point $P(3,-2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2 x$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b0f9bb8-9802-5c08-ac17-d465c6cdfcd9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518842 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(3,-2,3)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,-2) & =2(3) \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(3,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-2)}(x-(3)) \\\\ z-(3) & =(6)(x-(3)) \\\\ z & =6 x - 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,-2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2 \\\\ z &=6 x - 15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518838 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518849 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518849 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,-10)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3 x$, find the