2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0) & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2(\\frac{1}{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $x-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563687 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563687 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563687 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563687 #edb/ref 1071 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563687 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e141666-84d5-5edd-9a4e-a7cb3e788f33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563687 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563730 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563730 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563730 #edb/ref 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563730 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd4791a4-a5ea-5e12-b1c8-f45a53372bd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563730 #edb/ref 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z=2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
